WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.420
INTRO:
Imagine you’re the CEO of a large Japanese

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:07.060
bank with hundreds of billions of dollars
under management. Your bank has been a leading

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.270
voice within the Japanese financial space
for decades, but the future doesn’t look

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:16.259
so bright. The bank has been stagnating for
years, and it’s just a matter of time until

00:00:16.259 --> 00:00:17.710
profits start to decline. So, what do you
do? Well, the obvious way to generate better

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:19.810
returns is to take on more risk and bet on
the future. So, you go ahead and invest into

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:24.890
every hype tech company you can think of.
Doordash, Uber, Lemonade, SoFi, Supercell,

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:30.140
TikTok, Tesla, Netflix, basically everything.
For a couple of years, you ride up the momentum

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:34.570
of these growth stocks and your bank starts
to return to its former glory. But then, one

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:41.020
of your risky investments WeWork jumps off
a cliff and you end up losing $8.9 billion.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:44.780
To make things worse, the pandemic rolls around
and it looks like all your risky investments

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.480
are about to get obliterated. A normal investor
would probably just not look at their portfolio

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:55.270
until the storms over, but you’re not a
normal investor, you’re a giant bank. So,

00:00:55.270 --> 00:01:00.100
you try to buy your way out of the situation
and pour gas on every stock you own. And in

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:05.509
just 1 year, you go from posting your biggest
loss in 14 years to posting the biggest profit

00:01:05.509 --> 00:01:10.549
in Japanese history. In fact, you make so
much profit that you become the 6th most profitable

00:01:10.549 --> 00:01:16.960
company in the world pulling in $56.46 billion.
The second highest bank on the list is Goldman

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.429
Sachs, and they only pulled in roughly ⅓
of your profit. So, here’s how SoftBank

00:01:21.429 --> 00:01:24.420
bought themselves out of the pandemic.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:30.539
HUMBLE BEGINNINGS:
Taking a look back, the entire story of Softbank

00:01:30.539 --> 00:01:36.990
circles back to one man named Masayoshi Son
who was born on August 11, 1967 in Kyushu

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:42.490
Japan. Masayoshi had a pretty modest childhood,
in fact, it wouldn’t be a stretch to say

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:47.159
that he was poor. His grandfather had made
a living as a miner and his father was making

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:51.630
a living selling fish and pigs. Some reports
even suggest that Masayoshi often slept with

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:57.130
the pigs when he was young. So clearly, Masayoshi
didn’t have it easy by any means, and this

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:02.200
motivated him to work extremely hard and hustle
as much as possible. When he was 16 for example,

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:07.039
Masayoshi looked up to a man named Den Fujita
who was the president of McDonald’s Japan.

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:11.720
Den apparently had a best selling book that
blew away Masayoshi, and Masayoshi was trying

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:16.320
to meet Den eversince. Masayoshi started off
by repeatedly calling his assistants in an

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.270
effort to get a meeting. He says that he called
Den’s assistants 60 times over many months,

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:25.360
and every time they told him that they would
ask Den, but they would never get back to

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.360
him. So, one day, Masayoshi decided to go
to McDonald’s headquarters in Tokyo and

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.880
ask an assistant in person. And sure enough,
Den would agree to meet Masayoshi right then

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:39.130
and there for 15 minutes. Den told Masayoshi
that if he was 16, he would go to America

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:43.510
to get his education and then do something
related to computers. Hearing this from his

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:47.510
idol, Masayoshi would instantly make the move.
He contacted some of his relatives who lived

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:52.110
in South San Francisco and arranged to move
in with them. In San Francisco, Masayoshi

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:56.810
attended Serramonte High School, but Masayoshi
had no intention of sticking around. Masayoshi

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:00.520
felt that he already knew everything in the
textbooks and that he didn’t need to go

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:05.320
to class. So, he petitioned to just take all
his final exams and get his high school diploma.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.980
Just three weeks later, Masayoshi aced all
his exams and graduated high school. Masayoshi

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:15.530
would go on to attend UC Berkeley where he
majored in economics. Academics was no longer

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:19.670
his primary focus in college though. Following
Den’s advice, Masayoshi attempted to create

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:23.810
something in the computer space. He committed
to coming up with one new idea per day so

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:28.030
that he could iterate through a bunch of ideas
and pick the best one. After several months

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:32.370
and over 250 ideas later, Masayoshi settled
on creating a translation device that could

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.600
translate between Japanese, English, and German.
He would end up selling this technology to

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:43.580
Sharp for $450,000 in 1978. But despite making
a substantial amount of cash, Masayoshi didn’t

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:47.720
drop out of college. He eventually finished
up his economics degree in 1980 and returned

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:52.490
to Japan. Now that he had money though, instead
of getting a job, Masayoshi started Softbank

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:54.880
in September of 1981.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.990
SOFTWARE COMPANY:
Despite the name SoftBank, the company actually

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:04.970
had nothing to do with banking or the financial
industry. The company was originally a software

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:09.810
distribution company and Masayoshi felt that
it was a bank for software, hence the name.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:14.110
SoftBank was the first PC software distributor
in Japan which gave them a decent advantage.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:17.829
But given the small nature of the software
business at the time, SoftBank needed other

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:23.160
sources of revenue. So, Masayoshi expanded
the company into the magazine space in 1982.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:29.130
They came out with two monthly magazines called
Oh! PC and OH! MZ. These magazines covered

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:32.740
the manufacturing and engineering side of
PCs as opposed to the consumer side which

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:37.370
was much more common. This unique coverage
made these magazines extremely popular and

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:41.870
it didn’t take long for SoftBank to become
the largest PC magazine publisher in Japan.

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:46.811
In the meantime, SoftBank accidentally became
more and more of an investment advisor. Given

00:04:46.811 --> 00:04:50.250
that SoftBank was publishing magazines that
covered the latest developments on the engineering

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:55.470
and manufacturing side of PCs, investors often
based their investments on these magazines.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:05:00.960
Masayoshi decided to lean into this and target
American investors as well. In March of 1994,

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.460
SoftBank expanded to the US where they specialized
in identifying internet related investment

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:10.280
opportunities. Just a few months later, SoftBank
would go half public through the OTC markets

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:16.090
and end up raising 20 billion yen or roughly
$175 million. SoftBank could use this cash

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:20.130
to make their own investments as opposed to
just recommending investments. And with that,

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:23.950
SoftBank took their first steps into becoming
an actual bank.

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:28.560
RIDING DOTCOM WAVE:
Around this same time, growth shifted away

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.800
from PC companies and into internet companies.
Masayoshi was well aware of this trend and

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:39.169
was determined to capitalize on it. In late
1995, he made an investment in Yahoo, and

00:05:39.169 --> 00:05:44.210
over the next year he would work with Yahoo
to launch Yahoo in Japan. Two years later,

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:48.139
SoftBank would go fully public by debuting
on the Tokyo stock exchange. Following the

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:52.400
IPO, SoftBank quickly became a pure holding
company, and they would make several investments

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.889
into internet companies. But by far their
biggest investment of all time came in 2000

00:05:56.889 --> 00:06:01.890
when they invested $20 million into Alibaba.
Ironically, they bought in at the peak of

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:06.430
the dotcom bubble, but despite that, their
$20 million investment has turned into wait

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:12.930
for it $110 billion. And that's after the
50% crash Alibaba experienced over the past

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:18.300
year. Alibaba has grown so large that it accounts
for 47% of SoftBank’s entire assets, so

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:23.440
this was no doubt a massive win for SoftBank.
But as the dotcom bubble came to a close,

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.460
Masayoshi adopted a more conservative investing
style which led him to the telecommunications

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:28.460
industry.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:30.450
TELECOMMUNICATIONS &amp; STAGNATION:
Starting in 2004, Softbank went on a telecommunications

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:35.520
buying spree. In July of 2004, they acquired
Japan Telecom company which got them into

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.419
the fixed line telecommunications business.
2 years later, they bought Vodafone which

00:06:39.419 --> 00:06:43.990
got them into the mobile communications business.
And in 2008, they worked with Apple to be

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:48.590
the exclusive provider of the iPhone 3g in
Japan. All these endeavors allowed Softbank

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:53.870
to grow through the early 2000s, but the rate
of growth was nowhere near the 1980s and 90s.

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:57.861
Despite this, Masayoshi continued with this
more conservative style into the 2010s. In

00:06:57.861 --> 00:07:02.680
July of 2013, SoftBank acquired 70% of Sprint,
and they held onto this purchase up until

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:06.650
last year when they sold it to T-Mobile. On
the flipside though, Softbank’s revenue

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:11.680
and profit went from slow growth to no growth.
Some years were better and some years were

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:16.460
worse, but for the most part, SoftBank profited
between $4 and $8 billion for 5 years straight

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:22.100
between 2012 and 2017. Masayoshi knew that
something had to be done, so he started something

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:24.490
called the vision fund in 2017.

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:29.419
VISION FUND:
Masayoshi looked back at what made SoftBank

00:07:29.419 --> 00:07:34.750
successful in the first place and the answer
was clear: outsized bets in emerging industries.

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:38.960
The bulk of Softbank’s exponential growth
came from PCs, software, and the internet.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:43.270
So, Masayoshi decided to return to the roots
of the company with the Vision fund. He put

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.490
$100 billion into the Vision Fund and focused
on investing into all of the up and coming

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:51.630
tech companies including Nvidia, Flipkart,
ARM, GM, Slack, OneWeb, all the companies

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:56.720
we mentioned at the beginning, and much much
more. Over the next few years, SoftBank’s

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.130
profits grew to $10 to $20 billion per year.
But then, as we discussed at the beginning,

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:04.660
WeWork happened. SoftBank quickly racked up
billions of dollars worth of losses in the

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:10.080
first quarter of 2020, and we all know what
came about in March of 2020. Losses quickly

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:14.030
compounded on each other and it didn’t take
long for SoftBank to be in the worst position

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:18.360
they’ve ever been in. But luckily for SoftBank,
they had an ace up their sleeve which was

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:20.140
options trading.

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:24.960
MARKET MANIPULATION:
In the summer of 2020, SoftBank went out and

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.600
bought $4 billion worth of options in Amazon,
Netflix, Tesla, Microsoft, Alphabet and 19

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:34.169
of the biggest tech companies in the world.
$4 billion alone would be enough to move these

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:38.070
stocks multiple percent, but since it was
options, their $4 billion purchase actually

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:42.640
translated to $50 billion worth of stock.
I don’t think I need to explain how powerful

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:47.399
this is. SoftBank was one of the key reasons
for the tech rally in summer of 2020 when

00:08:47.399 --> 00:08:52.710
the entire Nasdaq rallied nearly 30% in 3
months. To put that in perspective, the market

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:58.350
generally grows 8% per year. SoftBank exited
their options positions at the end of 2020,

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:02.310
but not before locking in tens of billions
of dollars worth of profits. When it was revealed

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:06.470
that SoftBank was the whale behind these massive
purchases, investors and the SEC were not

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:11.051
happy. The SEC launched an investigation into
SoftBank, but we don’t have a decision yet.

00:09:11.051 --> 00:09:16.620
The truth is though, the SEC is unlikely to
do anything to SoftBank because they probably

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:20.720
didn’t break any laws. Here’s the thing,
it’s not illegal to buy $4 billion worth

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:25.370
of options. Now, if the SEC is able to dig
up some sort of insider trading evidence or

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:29.810
evidence of a pump and dump, then they could
fine SoftBank. But, if all SoftBank did was

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:35.030
buy a crap load of options, it’s not illegal
and the SEC can’t do anything about it.

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:39.200
Even if the SEC did somehow fine SoftBank
for hundreds of millions or even a few billion,

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.520
it would be nothing in comparison to the $50
billion they made. So, there’s no doubt

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:47.830
that SoftBank came out on top financially.
Morally, however, it’s a different story.

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:52.440
Do you guys think SoftBank should be punished?
